"""dosplex: install, update, start, stop and remove DosPlex on stock SteamOS.

Run it as root (sudo) on the device, from Desktop mode or over ssh:

    dosplex install BUNDLE --manifest SHA256 [--allow-untested] [--main-hid P] [--guest-hid P] [--output-mode W H HZ]
    dosplex install --release ARCHIVE [--signature FILE] [...]      a signed release instead of a bundle
    dosplex update [--channel URL] [--check] [--allow-untested]     the newest signed release for this device
    dosplex rollback                                                 back to the release before this one
    dosplex start [--layout single|vertical|horizontal] [--boot]     controllers are optional at start
    dosplex stop | status | repair | version | preflight
    dosplex autostart on|off|status                                  start DosPlex at every boot (experimental; off after an install)
    dosplex entry on|off|status                                      the "DosPlex" entry for Steam's library (on after an install)
    dosplex control ACTION [ARGUMENT...]                             the versioned JSON interface for user interfaces
    dosplex library on|off|slot|release|arrive|eject|share|status    the shared game library (experimental)
    dosplex uninstall [--remove-guest]

What it does, and the rules it keeps (ADR-005, ADR-001):

  * The CLI is the verifier. It hashes every bundle file itself (O_NOFOLLOW, per file) against the
    manifest whose SHA-256 you approved, before anything from the bundle is executed. Code from the
    bundle never takes part in that check.
  * A signed release (T-702) needs no digest: its manifest carries a minisign signature that this program checks
    with its own pure-Python Ed25519 and the key pinned in RELEASE_KEYS, and then every file by the manifest.
    `update` reads a signed release index (default: the GitHub releases, or --channel / DOSPLEX_CHANNEL; an
    https:// or file:// address) and installs the newest release above the installed one that this device is
    tested for. `rollback` switches back to the release that install and update keep.
  * The payload goes to /opt/dosplex/releases/<version>/ with an atomic /opt/dosplex/current symlink and
    one previous release kept. State and the write-ahead journal live in /var/lib/dosplex/ (the journal
    directory is root-only, mode 0700, and never a symlink).
  * Guest is created with `useradd --create-home --user-group` (home 0700, no extra groups) or an
    existing Guest is adopted. Every change is journaled before it is made: intent, apply, done.
    `dosplex repair` completes or rolls back an interrupted operation; `dosplex uninstall` replays the
    journal in reverse.
  * start and stop wrap the retained trial that runs today. They run as a transient system service
    (systemd-run), so an ssh logout cannot kill them. A reboot returns the device to stock SteamOS
    unless `dosplex autostart on` wired one unit into boot; `dosplex start` brings DosPlex back.
  * Nothing is written under /usr, steamos-readonly and pacman are never run, no display-manager unit
    is touched, and no group is granted. These rules are enforced in code (see Env and check_command).

Every filesystem root and external command is injectable: --sysroot (default /) moves all paths, and the
commands are looked up on PATH, so tests run the whole program against a fake tree and fake commands.
Only with a non-default sysroot, tests may also use DOSPLEX_TEST_ROOT_UID (which uid counts as root),
DOSPLEX_TEST_CRASH_AT=<point> (exit abruptly at a named journal point, see Env.fault), DOSPLEX_TEST_FAULT_LOG
(append the points that were reached), DOSPLEX_TEST_TTY (treat stdin as a terminal) and the
short waits DOSPLEX_TEST_IDLE_SECONDS, DOSPLEX_TEST_DISPLAY_SECONDS, DOSPLEX_TEST_STEAM_SECONDS and
DOSPLEX_TEST_GUEST_SECONDS.
"""
